# Cursor Rules for audio-channel-queue

## Project Overview
This is a TypeScript npm package for audio queue management with real-time progress tracking and event handling.

## Code Standards
- Use TypeScript with strict mode enabled
- All public functions must have comprehensive JSDoc documentation with examples
- All variables and functions should show a return type
- Object keys and imports should be alphabetically sorted
- Interfaces and types should be alphabetically sorted
- CSS rules should be alphabetically sorted
- CSS styling should be accomplished with as few rules as possible
- Use modular architecture - keep concerns separated across files
- Return all time values in milliseconds for consistency
- Use consistent error handling with try/catch blocks
- Export interfaces and types from src/types.ts

## File Organization
- src/types.ts - All TypeScript interfaces and type definitions
- src/core.ts - Core queue management functions
- src/info.ts - Audio information and progress tracking
- src/events.ts - Event handling and emission logic
- src/utils.ts - Helper functions and utilities
- src/index.ts - Main entry point with organized exports

## Testing Guidelines
- Write comprehensive tests for all new functionality
- Use Jest with jsdom environment for HTMLAudioElement testing
- Maintain 80%+ code coverage
- Include both positive and negative test cases
- Test error handling and edge cases

## Documentation
- Update CHANGELOG.md for all changes following semantic versioning
- Keep README.md examples current and functional
- Include migration guides for breaking changes
- Use JSDoc @example tags with realistic usage scenarios
- For JSDocs fileoverview don't put in the author or version number

## Naming Conventions
- Use camelCase for functions and variables
- Use PascalCase for interfaces and types
- Use descriptive names that clearly indicate purpose
- Prefix callback types with the event name (e.g., ProgressCallback)

## Dependencies
- Minimize external dependencies
- Justify any new dependencies in commit messages
- Prefer native JavaScript/TypeScript solutions when possible

## Backwards Compatibility
- Maintain existing public API contracts
- Add new features as optional parameters with defaults
- Provide migration guides for any breaking changes
- Test against existing usage patterns 